lessons.body:
Structs and enums are value types; classes are reference types. Value types are copied on assignment — mutations on a copy don't affect the original. Classes share identity: two variables can point to the same instance.

```swift
struct Point { var x: Int }
var a = Point(x: 1)
var b = a
b.x = 2 // a.x is still 1 — independent copy

class Box { var value = 1 }
let box1 = Box()
let box2 = box1
box2.value = 99 // box1.value is also 99 — same instance
```

The key mental model: value types give you safety through isolation; reference types enable shared mutable state. Swift's standard library types (Array, String, Dictionary) are all value types — they use copy-on-write to keep performance acceptable, so an assignment is cheap until one side actually mutates.

When to choose struct: modeling data, prefer predictability, default choice in Swift. When to choose class: need identity, inheritance, Objective-C interop, or shared mutable state.

An `Optional<T>` is an enum with two cases: `.some(T)` wrapping a value, and `.none` representing absence. Swift forces you to acknowledge the possibility of nil before using a value.

```swift
var name: String? = "Alice"

// Safe unwrapping
if let n = name { print(n) }
guard let n = name else { return }
let length = name?.count       // optional chaining → Int?
let display = name ?? "Guest" // nil-coalescing → String

// Optional map
let upper = name.map { $0.uppercased() } // Optional("ALICE")

// Unsafe — only when you are certain it's non-nil
let forced = name! // crashes at runtime if name is nil
```

Prefer `if let` or `guard let` for control flow, optional chaining for property/method access, and nil-coalescing for providing defaults. Force unwrap (`!`) is acceptable only when a nil value would represent a genuine programmer error — for example, loading a bundled asset that must exist.

`compactMap` is the collection-level equivalent: it maps and discards nils in one pass, returning a non-optional array.

Protocols define contracts — a list of requirements (methods, properties, initializers) that conforming types must satisfy. Extensions let you add default implementations, enabling protocol-oriented programming without inheritance.

```swift
protocol Greetable {
    var name: String { get }
    func greet() -> String
}

extension Greetable {
    func greet() -> String { "Hello, \(name)!" } // default impl
}

struct User: Greetable { let name: String }
print(User(name: "Alice").greet()) // "Hello, Alice!"
```

Generics let you write algorithms once and use them with any type that satisfies constraints:

```swift
func largest<T: Comparable>(_ a: T, _ b: T) -> T {
    return a > b ? a : b
}
```

`some Protocol` (opaque type) tells the compiler the concrete type is fixed but hidden — great for return types in SwiftUI views. `any Protocol` (existential) erases the concrete type entirely, enabling heterogeneous collections at the cost of dynamic dispatch.

Prefer composition (multiple small protocols) over deep inheritance hierarchies. Protocols + generics + extensions form the backbone of Swift's standard library design.

